# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the parts you need.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Everything here is optional. With no .env at all the signalling server runs on
# STUN only, which is enough for two tabs on this machine and for two people on
# the same wifi. It is NOT enough to invite someone across the internet — read
# the TURN section before you do that.
#
# Node reads this file natively (process.loadEnvFile), so there is no dotenv
# dependency and no import to add. Restart `npm run server` after editing it.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Basics
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Port the signalling server listens on. Vite proxies /api and /ws here during
# `npm run dev`; if you change it, vite.config.js reads the same variable.
#
# NOT NAMED `PORT`, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. `PORT` means "the port the process
# reading me should listen on" — Docker sets it, every PaaS sets it, and so do
# several dev-tool launchers, all of them addressing Vite. When this variable was
# called PORT, any of them made Vite proxy /api and /ws back to itself: both
# pages loaded, built the world, showed a room code, and never saw each other,
# with nothing in any console to say why. Two processes, two meanings, one name.
#
# `server/index.js` still falls back to PORT when SIGNALLING_PORT is unset, so a
# production host that only knows PORT keeps working — there is no Vite in that
# picture to be confused by it.
SIGNALLING_PORT=5181

# How many people fit in one room. Voice is a peer-to-peer mesh, so everybody
# uploads their voice to everybody else: cost per person is (N-1) x ~48 kbit/s.
# Eight is about 340 kbit/s of upload, which a home connection carries without
# noticing. Raising this past ~10 will start to break the person with the
# weakest upstream, and it will look like *everyone else* is glitching.
#
# SCREEN SHARING CHANGES THIS ARITHMETIC ENTIRELY, for one person at a time.
# A shared screen is capped at 2.2 Mbit/s and 30 fps (src/net/mesh.js), so the
# person presenting to a full room of eight is uploading up to 15 Mbit/s while
# everybody else is still uploading 340 kbit/s. That is a normal home upstream
# and it is also the reason the cap exists: left to negotiate freely a 1080p60
# desktop asks for 8 Mbit/s, which at eight people is 56 Mbit/s, and the way
# that fails is that the uplink saturates, the bufferbloat lands on the same
# queue as everybody's voice, and the room concludes the VOICE CHAT is broken.
#
# Screen audio and the picture are both given lower network priority than
# speech, so on a link that cannot keep up the film degrades and the
# conversation does not.
MAX_ROOM_SIZE=8


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TURN — the relay. Read this bit.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# STUN lets two browsers find each other directly. It fails for roughly one
# connection in six: symmetric NAT, most mobile carriers, and essentially every
# corporate or university network. When it fails, the affected person joins the
# room, sees everybody walking around, and hears nothing. Nothing errors and
# nothing logs — it is indistinguishable from everyone else being quiet.
#
# So: configure TURN before you invite anybody who is not on your own wifi.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Option A — Cloudflare Realtime. This is the one to use.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   1. https://dash.cloudflare.com  ->  Realtime  ->  TURN
#   2. "Create TURN key". Name it anything.
#   3. It shows you a **Key ID** and a **Key Token**, once. Paste them below.
#
# The free tier is 1,000 GB of relayed traffic a month. Voice is about 48
# kbit/s per stream, so that is roughly 46,000 person-hours — you will not
# reach it.
#
# The token below never reaches a browser. The server exchanges it for a
# credential that expires in two hours, caches that, refreshes it at 75% of its
# life, and hands the browser only the short-lived half (server/ice.js).
CF_TURN_KEY_ID=
CF_TURN_API_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Option B — your own coturn, with expiring credentials.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TURN_SECRET is coturn's `static-auth-secret`, with `use-auth-secret` enabled.
# The server derives a two-hour username/password pair from it per player; the
# secret itself stays here.
TURN_URL=
TURN_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Option C — a TURN server with fixed credentials. Last resort.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are handed to the browser verbatim, so they are public the moment one
# player opens devtools, and they do not expire. Anyone who takes them can relay
# traffic on your bill. Use A or B if you possibly can.
TURN_USERNAME=
TURN_CREDENTIAL=

# Comma-separated STUN servers. Ignored when Cloudflare is configured, because
# its response already bundles its own.
STUN_URLS=stun:stun.l.google.com:19302,stun:stun1.l.google.com:19302


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The jukebox's paste-a-link feature
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Pressing U at the jukebox streams audio from a pasted YouTube link
# (server/youtube.js). This needs `yt-dlp` on the server's PATH — it is a
# separate system binary, not an npm package, so `npm install` does not get it
# for you:
#
#     winget install yt-dlp.yt-dlp        (Windows)
#     brew install yt-dlp                 (macOS)
#     pip install -U yt-dlp                (anywhere with Python)
#
# The server logs whether it found `yt-dlp` in its startup banner. Without it,
# everything else still works — pasting a link just fails with a toast instead
# of playing.
#
# yt-dlp needs occasional updates to keep working at all: YouTube regularly
# changes internal details specifically to break scrapers, and a stale install
# will eventually start failing for reasons unrelated to anything in this repo.
#
# This is a private/local feature. Downloading YouTube audio server-side is
# against YouTube's terms of service — an accepted trade-off for a project
# that is not being publicly deployed or distributed.

# Longest video, in seconds, `resolve` will accept. A policy choice, not a
# technical limit — raise it if you trust whoever has the invite link.
YOUTUBE_MAX_DURATION_S=900

# Only needed if the server logs "yt-dlp NOT FOUND" despite `yt-dlp --version`
# working fine in your own terminal. Node's spawn does its own PATH search on
# Windows and it can miss an install `where.exe` finds without trouble —
# happened on this project's own dev machine with a WinGet install. Point this
# at the exe directly and it's bypassed entirely:
#
#     where.exe yt-dlp        (Windows — copy the path it prints)
#     which yt-dlp            (macOS/Linux)
YOUTUBE_YTDLP_PATH=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Going live
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Browsers only grant microphone access on a **secure origin**. localhost counts,
# which is why two tabs on this machine work with none of the above. Anybody
# else needs HTTPS, which means one public HTTPS name pointed at this process:
#
#     npm run build
#     SIGNALLING_PORT=5181 node server/index.js --prod
#
# `--prod` makes the same process serve the built client, so the page and the
# WebSocket share one origin and an invite link is the whole configuration.
# Put Caddy, a Cloudflare Tunnel, ngrok or any reverse proxy in front of it for
# the certificate — a tunnel is the fastest and needs no DNS.
#
# The invite is then just the URL with the room code on it, exactly as the game
# puts it on screen when you press J:
#
#     https://your-name.example/?room=pxk-mwe-q7t
